As `spatial_similarity()` quantifies spatial similarity in terms of how often
labels neighbor one another, its output depends on which spots/cells are
considered neighbors. In `friendlycells`, we provide three
[standard](https://squidpy.readthedocs.io/en/stable/modules/gr/spatial_neighbors.html)
ways to define neighbor relationships between spots/cells:

- `"radius"`: the neighbors of a spot/cell are all others within `radius` of
  it.
- `"knn"`: the neighbors of a spot/cell are the `k` spots/cells closest to it.
  We ensure that neighborhoods are symmetric, so a spot/cell can have more than
  `k` neighbors if it is among the nearest neighbors of many others.
- `"delaunay"`: the spots/cells surrounding each one, by Delaunay
  triangulation, ignoring connections longer than `radius`.

The default setting in `spatial_similarity()`, `"auto"`, represents our
recommendation without any knowledge of the data being investigated: it uses
`"radius"` if spots/cells lie on a grid and uses `"delaunay"` if they instead
represent segmented cells.

The plots below show the impact of neighborhood definitions using
`plot_neighborhoods()`. To visualize the neighborhoods in your own data, call
`plot_neighborhoods()` with the same `samples`, `neighborhood`, `radius` and
`k` as you want to use in `spatial_similarity()`. In each plot,
`plot_neighborhoods()` highlights a small number of spots/cells in a solid
color and colors their neighbors in a paler variant.

The plots use the same generated tissue section, with holes and a narrow
tear, laid out as your choice of data. Choose your language and data to
see the plots for them:

