## Delaunay

As Delaunay triangulation connects a spot/cell to its neighbors in all
directions (even if one direction is far away), it links each one to those
surrounding it, whatever their spacing. This can, however, lead to undesirable
connections stretching across large gaps. The `radius` parameter limits how
far these connections can stretch: the default (3 times the typical spacing)
keeps those across small holes, and an infinite radius keeps every connection
regardless of the size of the gap it spans.

::: {.grid-hex}
On the hexagonal grid, Delaunay triangulation yields the six immediate
neighbors when they exist. A radius of 150 µm prevents connections beyond
them, and the default is 300 µm.
:::

::: {.grid-square}
Delaunay triangulation splits each square into two triangles along an
arbitrary diagonal. A `radius` of 10 µm drops the diagonals, leaving the four
side neighbors, but then `"delaunay"` is the same as the default `"radius"`;
with larger radii the diagonals are arbitrary and connections cross gaps, so
avoid `"delaunay"` for bins.
:::

::: {.grid-segmented}
Here, the default radius is about 21 µm.
:::

::: {.lang-r .grid-hex}

::: {.cell layout-ncol="3"}

